In 2016, five states parties specifically called for strengthening Protocol III: Argentina, Costa Rica, Croatia, the Holy See, and Moldova.[90] At least three of these states supported an effects-based definition of incendiary weapons in particular. Croatia recognized the problems with the current definitional loophole and called to improve “Protocol III’s definitions and scope by focusing on actual effects of the weapon, and not its intended effects.”[91] Moldova was similarly concerned about “the consequences of weapons with incidental incendiary effects,” and called on states parties to begin work in 2017 to address the “legal loopholes around use in order to fully ensure the continued applicability and relevance of Protocol III.”[92] The Holy See declared that incendiary weapons should be “regulated or prohibited regardless of the purpose for which the weapons are primarily designed.”[93],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
